### Tested versions

- Reproducible in 4.6.2.stable.mono

### System information

Godot v4.6.2.stable.mono - Ubuntu 24.04.5 LTS 24.04 on Wayland - X11 display driver, Multi-window, 2 monitors - Vulkan (Forward+) - integrated Intel(R) UHD Graphics 620 (WHL GT2) - Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-8265U CPU @ 1.60GHz (8 threads) - 15.29 GiB memory

### Issue description

When calling `OptionButton.GetItemIndex(int val)` with val = `-1`  returns -1 regardless of the button having an  entry with '-1' as id.

This is a bug as neither the `OptionButton` class documentation or either  the `GetItemIndex`/`AddItem` method documentations mention this behaviour.

Expected behaviour: OptionButton.GetItemIndex(-1) returns the index of the item with '-1' as id if there is one, and -1  otherwise.

### Steps to reproduce

Using GdUnit4Test, the issue can be simply tested with the following snippet : 

```cs
using GdUnit4;
using Godot;
using static GdUnit4.Assertions;
namespace UnitTests4Godot.test;

using MarchingTrianglesTerrain.addons.marchingTriangles;


[TestSuite]
[RequireGodotRuntime]
public class TestIssue
{

    [GdUnit4.TestCase]
    public void Test()
    {
        var button = new OptionButton();
        button.AddItem("Hello World!",10);
        button.AddItem("Hello World!",-10);
        button.AddItem("Hello World!",-1);
        button.AddItem("Hello World!",5);

        var idx = button.GetItemIndex(10);
        AssertThat(idx).IsEqual(0);

        idx = button.GetItemIndex(5);
        AssertThat(idx).IsEqual(3);

        idx = button.GetItemIndex(-10);
        AssertThat(idx).IsEqual(1);

        idx = button.GetItemIndex(-1);
        AssertThat(idx).IsEqual(2);
    }
}
```

